  <text>
    <body>
      <p>Chinese residents appear in the precinct's directories from the 1870s. The first are listed by a single name, in the form the directory compilers used for Chinese men: Ah Take at No. 13 <ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref> in 1873, and Ah Fin at No. 62 <ref target="civic?id=Bouverie%20Street">Bouverie Street</ref> in 1877. More appear after 1900, when Ah Hem ran a Chinese laundry at No. 30 <ref target="civic?id=Sydney%20Road">Sydney Road</ref> (1905), and Ah Quong, Ah Chuck, Ah Coon, Ah Sim, Ah Sing and Ah Toon are listed in <ref target="civic?id=Berkley%20Street">Berkley Street</ref>, Bouverie Street, <ref target="civic?id=Leicester%20Street">Leicester Street</ref>, Madeline Street and <ref target="civic?id=Lincoln%20Place">Lincoln Place</ref> between 1910 and 1920. Kee Ow was a cabinet maker at No. 187 <ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref> in 1910.</p>
      <p>Some addresses stayed with Chinese occupants over several directories. No. 219 Queensberry Street was Loon Yick Louey's in 1915 and Louey Lom Yick's, as a dealer, in 1920 and 1925, and is listed simply as "Chinese" in 1930 and 1935. No. 30 Sydney Road was Ah Hem's laundry in 1905 and is listed as a "Chinese lndry" again in 1930. At No. 73 Madeline Street William Chong (1915) was followed by C. Wing Shing's laundry (1920). Paul Chin lived at No. 107 <ref target="civic?id=Barry%20Street">Barry Street</ref> from 1920 to 1935, and Phebe Chin, a teacher, is on the 1928 electoral roll at the same address.</p>
      <p>From 1920 the directories often list a house as "Chinese" with no name at all, as they did for some Italian and Greek households. These entries are in <ref target="civic?id=Cumberland%20Place">Cumberland Place</ref> (1920), Barry Street, Berkley Street, Bouverie Street and Queensberry Street (1930s), and along <ref target="civic?id=Swanston%20Street">Swanston Street</ref>, where No. 472 was a Chinese herbalist in 1935 and No. 498 a Chinese laundry from 1935 to 1945.</p>
      <p>The table is drawn only from the directories and lists each entry as printed. It includes listings named as "Chinese" and people whose names suggest they were Chinese; a name alone can mislead, so some may not have been. People named in the electoral rolls are not included in the table. Numbers changed when streets were renumbered, so the same number in different decades may not be the same building.</p>
